Influenza A/B
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Abrupt onset high fever/chills, Severe debilitating myalgia/prostration, Dry continuous hacking cough, Distinct winter seasonal occurrence

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-022
Reason for Visit: Extremely abrupt onset of high fever, severe prostration, and respiratory distress requiring urgent assessment for viral pneumonitis or secondary bacterial pneumonia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly/vulnerable patient with sudden, debilitating fever, severe chest pain with coughing, and an inability to get out of bed. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate respiratory compromise and rule out acute secondary complications prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It hit me like a truck two hours ago. I'm freezing, every muscle in my back is agonizing, and I can't stop this dry cough."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced extremely abruptly 12 hours ago with a sudden onset of high fever (39.5C) and severe, debilitating chills. The patient reports profound, severe generalized myalgia, particularly affecting the back and long muscles of the legs. The patient describes extreme systemic fatigue, intense prostration, and profound physical weakness forcing them to bed. A dry, severe, and continuous hacking non-productive cough is present. Patient also notes a distinct prominent severe headache and excruciating retro-orbital pain. Complete absence of a prominent, severe productive cough. It is currently mid-winter (late July).
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely unable to perform ADLs due to severe prostration and muscle pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is elderly and struggling to provide adequate care and hydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 39.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Acutely toxic appearance, flushed, shivering under blankets, coughing forcefully and continuously.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals tachypnoea and harsh bronchial breath sounds globally, but no focal crepitations or dullness to percussion to suggest lobar consolidation. Cardiovascular exam shows regular tachycardia. Neurological exam reveals an alert patient with no nuchal rigidity. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Abrupt onset high fever/chills, Severe debilitating myalgia/prostration, Dry continuous hacking cough, and Distinct winter seasonal occurrence.
Environmental Observations: Room is cold; spouse advised on appropriate heating while avoiding overheating the febrile patient.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Unvaccinated for seasonal influenza this year.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Influenza A/B (Acute Phase) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

COVID-19 - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable without a swab, though the extreme abruptness ("hit like a truck") and severe myalgia are highly classic for Influenza. A multiplex PCR is required.

Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a productive, purulent cough and the lack of focal lobar signs (crackles, dullness) on auscultation.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The severe headache lacks accompanying nuchal rigidity or photophobia, and the prominent dry cough points to a respiratory primary source.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic, severe acute seasonal influenza characterized by extreme prostration, high fever, and a harsh dry cough. Given the abrupt severity of the symptoms, the tachycardia, and the patient's vulnerable demographic, a direct clinical examination was mandatory to confidently exclude acute secondary bacterial pneumonia, assess baseline oxygenation, and rule out impending respiratory failure. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of undetected hypoxic deterioration, wholly justifying the urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe strict, scheduled antipyretics (alternating paracetamol and ibuprofen). Advise immediate commencement of oral Oseltamivir (75mg twice daily for 5 days) if within 48 hours of symptom onset. Request a multiplex respiratory PCR swab via a pathology home-visit or drive-through clinic tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the antipyretic strictly every 4 hours rather than waiting for the fever to spike, the patient will maintain a steady state of temperature control; this will reward them with significantly reduced muscle aching and shivering, reinforcing adherence to the medication schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Emesis bags, bedside water jug.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review in 24 hours to monitor SpO2 trajectory and fever response.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops sharp, stabbing chest pain with deep breaths, starts coughing up thick green or bloody sputum, experiences severe shortness of breath at rest, or becomes confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending multiplex PCR swab; respiratory status is dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme fever and muscle aches typically peak over the first 3 days, but the profound fatigue and dry cough can persist for 2-3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The influenza virus severely damages the lining of your lungs, making you highly susceptible to a secondary bacterial pneumonia. If your cough suddenly becomes productive with colored phlegm or your breathing becomes rapid and shallow, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has caused a massive inflammatory response, which is why your muscles ache so severely. The antiviral medication (if started early) stops the virus from releasing newly made copies into your body, potentially shortening the severe phase by a day or two.
Actionable Steps: Alternate your fever medications strictly. Do not attempt to push through the fatigue; absolute bed rest is required. Isolate from other household members.

